  "interpretation": [
    "Runtime policy selects inferred pipeline depth at one fixed launch; launch-configuration selection remains a separate experiment-harness operation. The reported end-to-end device speedups combine those two decisions.",
    "Baseline and selected plans independently select from the same four launch configurations. The baseline receives the same alias contracts and no contract LICM; all retained baseline variants have zero async-copy sites.",
    "BF16 rotary is not uniformly a no-op: w4/w8 have identical binaries across depths and skip timing, while w1/w2 produce pipelines. The external configuration search selects the profitable w1/stages4 plan.",
    "Resize selects ordinary loads at its fastest launch. Other slower launch configurations can select profitable pipelines relative to their own weaker baselines without beating the independently tuned fastest baseline."
  ],
  "limits": [
    "Bounded selected workload study on one B300; four timed workloads represent three kernel families. It is not a random corpus survey or a universal regression-avoidance guarantee.",
    "Three fresh input seeds validate the frozen decisions; there is only one cold selection per launch configuration. Repeated independent calibration choices under changing load/clocks were not measured.",
    "Timings measure warmed CUDA graph device execution with 32 invocations per sample and15 shuffled confirmation rounds. Compilation, calibration, host metadata guards/dispatch and allocation are excluded; gains require amortization.",
    "Calibration depends on example contents and warm-cache/clock regime. A 2% margin is an engineering threshold, not a statistical confidence bound.",
    "Plans bind exact scalar values, tensor shapes/strides/dtypes/device and alignment plus grid. Replacement tensors with that metadata are tested; changed metadata requires a new plan and is not covered by these timing claims.",
    "All performance kernels fully overwrite outputs and use replay_safe=True. Reset-inclusive calibration for accumulating state and optional LICM combinations require the separate runtime tests; this performance study does not measure them.",
    "Explicit source schedules are preserved, so AITER RMSNorm is excluded. Single-iteration inference pruning is not established by these real-kernel timings; targeted compiler/runtime tests cover that rule.",
    "Boundary cases receive one validation input and calibration, without independent performance confirmations; their calibration timings are not included as speedup claims."
  ]
}
